    "created_at": "2020-10-14T14:30:49Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-10-14T14:30:49Z",
    "body": "\r\n> * The iframe is cross-origin to its parent (or the appropriate equivalent spec language) and is not visible to the user\r\n> * The iframe is skipped [link to css-contain spec]\r\n> * The user agent believes it is preferable to skip updating the rendering\r\n\r\nI think what @chrishtr said is pretty good. The css-contain for content-visibility iframes says that the UA should skip the whole loop if possible. The second point here is a stronger language than that, which implies that the UA must not do the work if the iframe is skipped. I think this language needs to be loosened a bit to be a \"should\" language. Specifically, we found that there are some situations (ie the first time iframe becomes skipped) that we can't skip the loop here, because we need to remove painted output.\r\n\r\nMaybe something along the lines of\r\n* The iframe is skipped [link to the css-contain spec] and UA does not need to update the iframe's rendering\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
